67:- **Snap barb, stops outward X.** The rear 7.2 mm of each bar (Z −15 to −7.8) is a tongue rooted at |X| 30, 1.6 thick, 17.8 long, ending at |X| 47.8. Its barb rises to Y 350, 1.1 mm past the slot ceiling at Y 348.9. The 90 deg catch face sits 0.2 mm from the web inner face, and the 4 mm ramp on the inboard side is the lead-in.
68:- **Detent pin, stops rearward Z.** The pin is now 0.8 tall, tip at |X| 50.9, with a 30 deg lead-in. It sits 0.6 mm into the 0.8 deep pocket, and 0.4 mm at the worst of the X float.
71:**Fitting.** The old rear slide-in does not work on the assembled frame: corner bracket br17 (|X| 51 to 55, Y 325.6 to 353.6, Z −70 to −50) sits against the web rear edge, so the lip cannot get round it. Instead, from the frame side with the side shell off, line the hook up at Z −15 to 5 and push it inward along X through the web slot, inner end first. The ramp presses the tongue down, and it clicks when the barb clears the web. The hook only clicks with the pin in its pocket, so the click also confirms Z. It can be fitted with the pack in or out.
73:**Release.** With a thin blade from the rear, over the pack top, press the tongue down just inboard of the web, then pull the hook out sideways. The tongue has 1.9 mm of room above the pack and needs 1.1. The pack slides in and out under the strap, so a pack swap does not need this.
75:**Print.** Stand each hook on its rear face (Z −15). Load paths then lie in the layer plane, the tongue is on the bed and the bar bridges 21.7 mm from the root to the lip, with no supports. Untested.
